Subject: [PATCH 3/3] configure: probe for .intel_syntax, fall back to the AT&T bignum mirror

Independent of 0001/0002 -- those touch m4/libtool.m4 and the generated
`configure`; this one touches `configure.ac` and
`crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64`. Any application order works.

libressl 4.3.2 imports 21 s2n-bignum routines as
`crypto/bn/arch/amd64/*.S`. They are the only Intel-syntax sources in the
tree, and tcc's assembler is AT&T-only -- it has no `.intel_syntax`
directive at all, so every one of the 21 fails with `unknown opcode
'.intel_syntax'`. That is why every recorded tcc build of libressl so far
has gone through `--disable-asm`, giving up *all* assembly (AES-NI, GHASH,
RC4, SHA, Montgomery multiplication) to work around a gap that affects
only the bignum subset.

`crypto/bn/arch/amd64/att/` already holds an AT&T translation of all 21,
generated by AWS's own upstream `attrofy.sed` and verified byte-identical
to the Intel originals when assembled by GNU as across 4 preprocessor
configs (see that directory's README.md and docs/native-tiers.md). It was
vendored but nothing pointed a build at it.

This patch wires it up as a capability probe rather than a compiler check.
`configure.ac` gains an AC_COMPILE_IFELSE that assembles

    .intel_syntax noprefix
    mov rax, rbx
    .att_syntax prefix

and defines AM_CONDITIONAL([ASM_INTEL_SYNTAX]) from the result;
`crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64` splits the 21 files into
ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL and ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT and selects between them
on that conditional. Both lists stay in EXTRA_DIST, so `make dist` is
unaffected either way; only the selected list reaches libcrypto_la_SOURCES.

Three properties this shape has that a compiler-name check would not:

- It asks the question that actually matters. Any assembler lacking the
  directive gets the mirror, not just tcc; any assembler that grows support
  later picks the originals back up with no change here.
- gcc and clang answer yes, so they keep compiling the Intel originals and
  their output is bit-for-bit what it was. The probe restores `.att_syntax`
  so assemblers that accept the directive are left in their original mode.
- The mirror stays a fallback rather than becoming the default, which is
  the role att/README.md states for it.

Note that the probe deliberately assembles a real Intel-syntax instruction
rather than the bare directive: recognising `.intel_syntax` and correctly
parsing Intel operand order are separate capabilities, and only the second
one makes the 21 files build.

This patch touches only `configure.ac` and `crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64`.
Regenerate the derived files after applying it with

    aclocal -I m4 && autoconf && automake

using autoconf 2.72 and automake 1.18.1 -- the versions that produced the
checked-in `configure` and `aclocal.m4`. Do NOT run `libtoolize`, or
`autoreconf` with `-i`/`-f`: the tree vendors libtool 2.4.2 macros that
0001 and 0002 patch in place, and libtoolize would replace them with
whatever libtool the build host has.

The conditional sits at the exact point in the file the 21 entries occupied
before the split, rather than being appended at the end. That is not
cosmetic. Appending reshuffles every object the linker sees after it, and a
gcc-built `libcrypto.so` then stops being byte-identical to one built
without this patch -- same instruction bytes, different addresses, with the
bignum and sha symbols interleaved differently. Both forms were built and
compared; the in-place form is what this patch uses.

With it, a gcc build of a `0003`-patched tree and a gcc build of an
unpatched tree are byte-identical, build-id included, when both are built
at the same path.

Getting to that statement took one detour worth recording, because it is
the kind of difference that is easy to wave away. Built at *different*
paths the two libraries differ, and the first differing byte lands in
`.note.gnu.build-id`. That is not a patch effect: two **byte-identical
pristine** trees built at two different paths reproduce exactly the same
pair of build-ids. libressl's build is not path-reproducible, with or
without this change. Underneath that, `.text`, the section headers, the
full symbol table and the file size all match.

diff --git a/dep/libressl/configure.ac b/dep/libressl/configure.ac
index c0ad0336..0208f39c 100644
--- a/dep/libressl/configure.ac
+++ b/dep/libressl/configure.ac
@@ -115,6 +115,29 @@ int main() {return 0;}
 AC_ARG_ENABLE([asm], AS_HELP_STRING([--disable-asm], [Disable assembly]))
 AM_CONDITIONAL([OPENSSL_NO_ASM], [test "x$enable_asm" = "xno"])
 
+# The s2n-bignum imports under crypto/bn/arch/amd64 are written in Intel
+# syntax.  Not every assembler understands the .intel_syntax directive --
+# tcc's built-in assembler is AT&T-only and rejects those files outright --
+# so probe for it and let crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64 fall back to the
+# AT&T mirror in crypto/bn/arch/amd64/att when it is missing.  The mirror is
+# generated from the same sources by upstream's attrofy.sed and assembles to
+# byte-identical objects under GNU as; see that directory's README.md.
+#
+# The probe restores .att_syntax so that assemblers which *do* accept the
+# directive are left in their original mode for the rest of the unit.
+AC_MSG_CHECKING([whether the assembler supports .intel_syntax])
+AC_COMPILE_IFELSE([AC_LANG_SOURCE([[
+__asm__(".intel_syntax noprefix\n\tmov rax, rbx\n\t.att_syntax prefix");
+int main(void) { return 0; }
+]])], [
+    asm_intel_syntax=yes
+    AC_MSG_RESULT(yes)
+], [
+    asm_intel_syntax=no
+    AC_MSG_RESULT(no)
+])
+AM_CONDITIONAL([ASM_INTEL_SYNTAX], [test "x$asm_intel_syntax" = "xyes"])
+
 # Conditionally enable assembly by default
 AM_CONDITIONAL([HOST_ASM_ELF_AARCH64],
     [test "x$HOST_ABI" = "xelf" -a "$host_cpu" = "aarch64" -a "x$enable_asm" != "xno"])
diff --git a/dep/libressl/crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64 b/dep/libressl/crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64
index 450e96ac..87ea178a 100644
--- a/dep/libressl/crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64
+++ b/dep/libressl/crypto/Makefile.am.elf-x86_64
@@ -7,27 +7,67 @@ ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/mont5-elf-x86_64.S
 ASM_X86_64_ELF += modes/ghash-elf-x86_64.S
 ASM_X86_64_ELF += rc4/rc4-elf-x86_64.S
 
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_add.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_cmadd.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_cmul.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_modadd.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_modsub.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_4_8.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_4_8_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_6_12.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_6_12_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_8_16.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_8_16_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_4_8.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_4_8_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_6_12.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_6_12_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_8_16.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_8_16_alt.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sub.S
-ASM_X86_64_ELF += bn/arch/amd64/word_clz.S
+# The s2n-bignum imports below are the only Intel-syntax sources in the
+# tree.  ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL is the upstream original; _BN_ATT is the
+# AT&T mirror (generated by upstream's attrofy.sed, byte-identical under
+# GNU as -- see bn/arch/amd64/att/README.md).  configure's ASM_INTEL_SYNTAX
+# probe picks between them, so an assembler without .intel_syntax support
+# still gets real assembly rather than needing --disable-asm.  Both lists
+# are distributed either way; only the selected one is compiled.
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL = bn/arch/amd64/bignum_add.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_cmadd.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_cmul.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_modadd.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_modsub.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_4_8.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_4_8_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_6_12.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_6_12_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_8_16.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_mul_8_16_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_4_8.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_4_8_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_6_12.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_6_12_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_8_16.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sqr_8_16_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/bignum_sub.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL += bn/arch/amd64/word_clz.S
+
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT = bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_add.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_cmadd.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_cmul.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_modadd.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_modsub.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_4_8.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_4_8_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_6_12.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_6_12_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_8_16.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_mul_8_16_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_4_8.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_4_8_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_6_12.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_6_12_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_8_16.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sqr_8_16_alt.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/bignum_sub.S
+ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT += bn/arch/amd64/att/word_clz.S
+
+# Appended here, at the position the 21 files occupied before they were
+# split out, so the object order handed to the linker is unchanged.  Putting
+# the selection at the end of the file instead is functionally equivalent but
+# reshuffles every symbol after it, which makes a gcc-built libcrypto.so
+# stop being byte-identical to one built without this patch for no reason.
+if ASM_INTEL_SYNTAX
+ASM_X86_64_ELF += $(ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL)
+else
+ASM_X86_64_ELF += $(ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT)
+endif
 
 ASM_X86_64_ELF += sha/sha1_amd64_generic.S
 ASM_X86_64_ELF += sha/sha1_amd64_shani.S
@@ -36,6 +76,8 @@ ASM_X86_64_ELF += sha/sha256_amd64_shani.S
 ASM_X86_64_ELF += sha/sha512_amd64_generic.S
 
 EXTRA_DIST += $(ASM_X86_64_ELF)
+EXTRA_DIST += $(ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_INTEL)
+EXTRA_DIST += $(ASM_X86_64_ELF_BN_ATT)
 
 if HOST_ASM_ELF_X86_64
 libcrypto_la_SOURCES += aes/aes_amd64.c
